    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §240, Dom Rel L; amd §575, Exec L; amd §4, Chap of 2022 (as proposed in S.6385-B & A.2375-C)",
    "lawSection": "Domestic Relations Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S860\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the domestic relations law, in relation to mandatory\ntraining of a forensic evaluator in relation to court ordered forensic\nevaluations involving child custody and visitation when the child is\nliving out-of-state; to amend the executive law, in relation to training\nin the prevention and intervention of domestic violence required for\nforensic investigators; and to amend a chapter of the laws of 2022\namending the domestic relations law and the executive law, relating to\ncourt ordered forensic evaluations involving child custody and visita-\ntion, as proposed in legislative bills numbers S. 6385-B and A. 2375-C,\nrelating to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2022,\nc.740, to clarify provisions relating to training of forensic custody\nevaluators.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation amends the underlying chapter by permitting a trained\nevaluator to conduct forensic evaluations remotely using video confer-\nencing when a child is living out of state and is more than 100 miles\nfrom the New York State border. The legislation now requires the Office\nfor the Prevention of Domestic Violence, as well as an organization\ndesignated by the Federal Department of Health and Human Services, to\nmutually develop and provide training. The organization designated by\nthe Federal Department of Health and Human Services replaced specific\nreferences to the New York State Coalition against Domestic Violence.\nLastly, the effective date has been changed from 180 days to one year to\nensure there is time for the training has to be developed and adminis-\ntered prior to the effective date to court operations are not impacted\nby lack of trained evaluators at the time of the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nNone.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is a negotiated change to the underlying chapter.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 740 of the Laws of 2022. S.6385-3 and\nA.2375-C both passed the Senate and Assembly, respectfully, in June 2022\nand February 2022.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal fiscal implications to the State or Local governments.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as a\nchapter of the laws of 2022 amending the domestic relations law and the\nexecutive law, relating to court ordered forensic evaluations involving\nchild custody and visitation, as proposed in legislative bills numbers\n5.6385-B and A.2375-C, takes effect.\n",
